$DIR/main.p:5:15: cannot pass the variadic arguments of keep to a task statement: the new task may outlive main's frame
    the variadic arguments of keep points into the variadic arguments of a call,
    in main's frame. A call with variadic arguments (at main.p:5) passes them in
    an array in main's frame, and the function gets a slice of that array. The
    task started here runs on its own, and may still be running after main
    returns and main's frame's memory is reclaimed.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Pass a value, which is copied, or an owner, which moves, instead of a view
    of the frame.
    Or start it with a sync.Scope, which waits for its tasks before its block
    ends, so that they may use main's variables:
        var s sync.Scope
        s.Task(func() { keep(1, 2) })
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3004" for a longer explanation)
